codex
Step 0 has exposed a serious issue in the paper’s own sharpness section: the displayed \(K_{k+3}\)-hub construction does not force \(k+1\) stem branch vertices when \(k\ge1\); a central star gives a spanning tree whose stem has only one branch vertex. I’m treating the main existence theorems separately, but I will not use that sharpness claim as a construction guarantee.

The most faithful positive candidate is now the \(k=0\) case: a Hamiltonian path is a spanning tree whose stem has no branch vertices, and line graphs provide claw-free instances. I’m testing whether the natural theorem-regime constructions survive ordinary greedy and line-graph/Euler attacks before deciding whether H is defensible.
