The strongest natural candidate also fails decisively. I used a diverse theorem-regime family of 246-vertex claw-free line graphs whose root graphs are unions of six edge-disjoint Hamilton cycles. The answer is known by composing those cycles, so G is clean; the graphs satisfy the paper’s \(k=0\) condition because no three vertices are pairwise at distance at least four. But a degree-aware greedy Hamilton-path heuristic solved 20/20 instances in a median 0.024 s (about 64,922 neighbor probes). This is exactly the sort of in-context attack that Track B requires to fail.

I’m documenting a Step-0 rejection rather than fabricating a hardness claim. The rejection will include the measured mechanical/compact costs, the failed Track A distribution claim, the failed Track B compression claim, and the counterexample to the paper’s sharpness construction.
